course: euphemisms
lesson: 30
title: Soft words for hard things: drinking and drugs
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Drinking too much alcohol and taking drugs can harm people, and talking about it plainly can feel embarrassing.
explain: So people use jokes and old slang for being drunk ("legless", "blotto", "squiffy") and cosy words for drinks ("a nightcap", "refreshments").
explain: Officials use cool, careful words instead: addiction becomes "substance abuse" or "substance misuse", and illegal drugs become "recreational drugs".
explain: A vivid example: "He's a social drinker" can be a gentle way of saying he drinks a lot, perhaps too much.
explain: Under the funny words there can be a real problem, so it helps to know exactly what they mean.
fact: "In his cups" is an old phrase meaning drunk.
fact: "Worse for wear" in Britain means drunk or badly hungover; a hangover is the headache and sick feeling the morning after drinking too much.
fact: A "liquid lunch" is a lunch of alcohol instead of food.
fact: "Refreshments" on an invitation can politely mean alcoholic drinks.
fact: A "nightcap" is an alcoholic drink before bed.
fact: "The hard stuff" means strong spirits such as whisky or vodka.
fact: "Hair of the dog" means an alcoholic drink taken to cure a hangover, which does not really work.
fact: "Feeling delicate" in Britain is a polite way to say hungover.
fact: "On the wagon" means not drinking alcohol.
fact: "Fell off the wagon" means started drinking again after stopping.
fact: "Dutch courage" is alcohol drunk to feel brave.
fact: A "social drinker" is someone who drinks regularly with others, sometimes too much.
fact: "Substance abuse" is the official phrase for addiction to drugs or alcohol; addiction means a person cannot stop taking something even though it harms them.
fact: "Substance misuse" is the British official phrase for harmful use of drugs or alcohol.
fact: "Recreational drugs" means illegal drugs taken for pleasure.
fact: "Party favours" is US slang for illegal drugs at a party, though it also means small party gifts.
fact: "Out of it" means very drunk or high on drugs (feeling strange and not in control because of a drug).
fact: "Blotto" is old British slang for very drunk.
fact: "Legless" is British slang for so drunk one cannot stand.
fact: "Squiffy" is old British slang for slightly drunk.
exercise: What does "in his cups" mean? | Drunk | Collecting cups | Washing up | Winning trophies
exercise: In Britain, what does "worse for wear" mean after a party? | Drunk or badly hungover | Wearing old clothes | Having a torn coat | Tired from dancing all night
exercise: What is a "liquid lunch"? | A lunch of alcohol instead of food | A bowl of hot soup for lunch | A smoothie for lunch | A lunch eaten outside in the pouring rain
exercise: An invitation says "Refreshments will be served". What can that politely mean? | Alcoholic drinks will be served | Cold water only, from a jug on the table | Fans will cool the room | People will get a rest
exercise: What is a "nightcap"? | An alcoholic drink before bed | A woolly hat for sleeping in | A bedtime story | A small pill that helps you sleep
exercise: What is "the hard stuff"? | Strong spirits like whisky or vodka | Difficult homework set for the weekend | Rock and stone | Tough meat
exercise: What is "hair of the dog"? | An alcoholic drink to cure a hangover | Fur left behind on the sofa by a pet | A dog-grooming tool | A lucky charm
exercise: A Briton says she is "feeling delicate" the morning after a party. What does she mean? | She is hungover | She feels like a flower | She has a broken bone | She is very polite
exercise: "Dad's on the wagon now." What does it mean? | He is not drinking alcohol | He is riding a cart pulled by horses | He works on a farm | He joined a band on tour
exercise: "Sadly, she fell off the wagon." What happened? | She started drinking again | She fell from a cart and hurt her arm | She failed a test | She left her job
exercise: What is "Dutch courage"? | Alcohol drunk to feel brave | Courage from the Netherlands | Bravery in war | A kind of cheese
exercise: What can it really mean when someone is called a "social drinker"? | He drinks regularly, maybe too much | He drinks only water at parties | He likes making friends | He works in a cafe
exercise: What is "substance abuse"? | Addiction to drugs or alcohol | Mistreating chemicals in a lab | Spilling liquids | Being rude to a teacher
exercise: In British official talk, what is "substance misuse"? | Harmful use of drugs or alcohol | Using glue for the wrong job at home | A mistake in science class | Wasting food
exercise: What are "recreational drugs"? | Illegal drugs taken for pleasure | Medicines given at a sports centre | Vitamins for playtime | Drinks sold at a park
exercise: At a wild US party, what can "party favours" mean? | Illegal drugs | Party hats only | Birthday cake | Thank-you cards
exercise: "He was totally out of it." What does it mean? | He was very drunk or high | He had left the room | He was not chosen for the team | He had run out of milk
exercise: In old British slang, what does "blotto" mean? | Very drunk | Covered in ink | Asleep in class | Very lucky
exercise: In British slang, what does "legless" mean? | So drunk one cannot stand | Having no legs | Very tired from walking | Sitting down all day
exercise: What does "squiffy" mean? | Slightly drunk | Very angry | A little wet | Squeezed tight
test: "Mum's feeling a bit delicate this morning, keep it down." Why should the kids be quiet? | Mum is hungover | Mum is sewing lace | Mum has a cold | Mum is reading
test: Grandad: "Just a small nightcap and then bed." What will he have? | An alcoholic drink before bed | A woolly hat | A cup of cocoa | A bedtime story
test: "Bloody hell, he's legless — get him a taxi." What is wrong with him? | He is so drunk he cannot stand | He has hurt both his legs playing football | He is too lazy to walk | He has lost his shoes
test: Press release: "The singer is receiving help for substance abuse." What is the singer's problem? | Addiction to drugs or alcohol | A sore throat that stops her singing | Too much work | Stage fright
test: "Had a pint of Dutch courage before his speech." Why did he drink? | To feel brave | To quench his thirst | Because he is Dutch | To celebrate the speech
test: "Uncle Jim's been on the wagon a year — no, two years now." Which is true? | Uncle Jim has not drunk alcohol for two years | Uncle Jim has driven a wagon for a year | Uncle Jim has not drunk alcohol for one year | Uncle Jim started drinking two years ago
test: Gossip: "The boss came back from a liquid lunch and fell asleep at his desk." What did the boss have? | Alcohol at lunch instead of food | Soup at lunch | A glass of milk | A long swim
test: "She drinks hair of the dog every Sunday, says it cures her head." What is she doing? | Drinking alcohol to cure a hangover | Brushing her dog's fur | Taking a medicine from the vet for her headache | Drinking herbal tea
test: Health leaflet: "Recreational drugs can damage your brain." What is the leaflet warning about? | Illegal drugs taken for pleasure | Sports drinks | Medicines that the doctor gives you to take | Sweets with sugar
test: "He fell off the wagon after the funeral." What happened? | He started drinking again | He fell from a cart at the funeral | He lost his way | He lost his job
test: "Bit squiffy after two glasses of wine, are we?" teases a friend. What is she saying? | You are slightly drunk | You are very drunk | You are not drunk at all | You are cold
test: "Lad was blotto by nine, sleeping on the stairs." How was the lad? | Very drunk | Covered in ink | Ill with the flu | Tired from football
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression "the hard stuff"? | It denotes strong spirits such as whisky or vodka | It denotes difficult examinations set at the end of the year | It denotes rock and stone | It denotes tough cuts of meat
exam: Identify the meaning of "substance misuse" as employed by official bodies in the United Kingdom. | The harmful use of drugs or alcohol | The improper use of adhesives | An error committed in a science lesson | The wastage of foodstuffs
exam: It is given that a gentleman is described as "in his cups". Select the response that is consistent with the established meaning of that expression. | He is drunk | He is collecting cups | He is washing dishes | He has won trophies
exam: Ascertain the meaning of "worse for wear" when it is applied to a person on the morning following a celebration. | Drunk or badly hungover | Attired in worn garments | Possessed of a torn coat | Fatigued from dancing
